# Section — a named block of a page: the heading, then the block. It is the
# composition of two things that were already here, `SectionHeader` and the stack
# rhythm, into the shape a page is actually written in —
#
#     use Section("Replies", count(r in Reply where r.tweet == id)):
#         for r in Reply where r.tweet == id by created desc limit 50:
#             use ReplyRow(...)
#
# — and the reason it is worth a facet is that the heading and its content stop
# being two siblings a host has to remember to keep together. Before `slot`, a
# section was a `use SectionHeader(...)` followed by a box the host indented
# correctly by hand; a section whose heading drifted onto the wrong block still
# compiled and still rendered.
#
# The subtitle is optional by value: pass "" and `SectionHeader` renders no
# subtitle line. It is `text` rather than `int` because it is usually a count and
# sometimes a sentence, and a count converts into text for free while a sentence
# does not convert into an int at all.
#
# THE COUNT IS PASSED AS AN EXPRESSION AND NOT AS A QUOTED, BRACED ONE, which is
# how this example used to read. A component argument is an expression, not a
# template: the quoted form is a string literal containing braces, and it used to
# compile clean and render the source text of the aggregate instead of its value,
# with nothing warning. The compiler refuses it now.
#
# `SectionHeader` owns the heading's own rules; this file owns nothing but the
# arrangement, which is why it has no `css:` block.
import "vocabulary.fct"
import "sectionheader.fct"
app FacetLayout:
    component Section(title: text, subtitle: text):
        box class "x-l-stack x-l-stack-tight":
            use SectionHeader(title, subtitle)
            slot
